Now I see the critical bug! Look at lines 5475-5488:

```c
if (NODE_TYPE(body) == NODE_STRING) {
    if (!IS_INFINITE_REPEAT(qn->lower) && qn->lower == qn->upper &&
        qn->lower > 1 && qn->lower <= EXPAND_STRING_MAX_LENGTH) {
      int len = NODE_STRING_LEN(body);

      if (len * qn->lower <= EXPAND_STRING_MAX_LENGTH) {
        int i, n = qn->lower;
        node_conv_to_str_node(node, body);  // line 5482: converts quantifier node to string node
        for (i = 0; i < n; i++) {
          r = node_str_node_cat(node, body);  // line 5484: concatenates body's string to node
          if (r != 0) return r;
        }
        onig_node_free(body);  // line 5487: frees body
        return r;
      }
    }
}
```

After `node_conv_to_str_node(node, body)` on line 5482, the node is converted from `NODE_QUANT` to `NODE_STRING`. The function `node_conv_to_str_node` does `xmemset(node, 0, sizeof(*node))` which zeros out the entire node, then sets the type to `NODE_STRING`. This means the quantifier-specific fields (like `qn->upper`, `qn->lower`, `qn->greedy`, etc.) are gone.

But wait - the function returns `r` (which is 0) after this, so `tune_quant` returns successfully. The node is now a `NODE_STRING`. So when `optimize_nodes` runs, it should hit the `NODE_STRING` case, not the `NODE_QUANT` case.

Let me think about this differently. The issue might be in `tune_tree` itself. Let me look at the `NODE_QUANT` case in `tune_tree`:

